1
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:12,543
At dusk, the last lamp on the street still

2
00:00:12,601 --> 00:00:13,808
waits for a hand.

3
00:00:14,400 --> 00:00:17,117
The lamplighter climbs, opens the glass,

4
00:00:17,175 --> 00:00:18,766
and the flame takes.

5
00:00:19,451 --> 00:00:21,540
At the end of the street, behind tall

6
00:00:21,610 --> 00:00:24,617
windows, another kind of light is burning:

7
00:00:25,209 --> 00:00:27,682
a hundred small lamps on a switchboard,

8
00:00:27,925 --> 00:00:29,934
and a voice behind each one.

9
00:00:30,538 --> 00:00:31,699
Hello, Central.

10
00:00:32,302 --> 00:00:33,823
Number, please.

11
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:51,029
A telephone call once needed a person in

12
00:00:51,076 --> 00:00:51,714
the middle.

13
00:00:52,411 --> 00:00:54,709
You lifted the receiver, an operator

14
00:00:54,768 --> 00:00:57,461
answered, and she connected you by hand,

15
00:00:57,635 --> 00:00:58,796
plug into jack.

16
00:00:59,400 --> 00:01:01,525
The machine that could do the job alone

17
00:01:01,571 --> 00:01:02,465
came early.

18
00:01:03,069 --> 00:01:07,295
In 1892, La Porte, Indiana, opened one of

19
00:01:07,341 --> 00:01:09,837
the first commercial automatic exchanges.

20
00:01:10,348 --> 00:01:12,415
The Bell System opened its first fully

21
00:01:12,473 --> 00:01:14,748
automatic office in 1921.

22
00:01:15,027 --> 00:01:17,918
But for decades, most calls still went

23
00:01:17,953 --> 00:01:18,940
through people.

24
00:01:20,758 --> 00:01:22,906
How many people did this work?

25
00:01:23,858 --> 00:01:26,226
Economists James Feigenbaum and Daniel

26
00:01:26,273 --> 00:01:28,200
Gross counted operators in the U.S.

27
00:01:28,223 --> 00:01:30,452
census, using the full records.

28
00:01:31,056 --> 00:01:34,005
In 1910, the telephone industry employed

29
00:01:34,040 --> 00:01:36,408
about seventy-three thousand operators.

30
00:01:36,919 --> 00:01:39,775
By 1930, about one hundred eighty-two

31
00:01:39,856 --> 00:01:40,576
thousand.

32
00:01:41,087 --> 00:01:42,259
That was the peak.

33
00:01:42,770 --> 00:01:45,603
By 1940, the count had fallen to about one

34
00:01:45,650 --> 00:01:47,461
hundred fifty-three thousand.

35
00:01:49,258 --> 00:01:51,139
What changed was the switch.

36
00:01:51,568 --> 00:01:55,295
Between 1920 and 1940, AT&T replaced

37
00:01:55,342 --> 00:01:57,606
operators with mechanical switching in

38
00:01:57,698 --> 00:01:59,138
over half of the U.S.

39
00:01:59,161 --> 00:02:00,264
telephone network.

40
00:02:00,938 --> 00:02:03,550
Among Bell telephones, the share on dial

41
00:02:03,666 --> 00:02:06,197
service rose from thirty-two percent in

42
00:02:06,255 --> 00:02:09,959
1930 to sixty percent in 1940.

43
00:02:10,620 --> 00:02:13,697
City by city, there was a night called the

44
00:02:13,755 --> 00:02:14,498
cutover.

45
00:02:15,195 --> 00:02:17,679
The switchboard went quiet, and the dial

46
00:02:17,761 --> 00:02:18,469
took over.

47
00:02:19,165 --> 00:02:22,927
Decades later, in 1965, Bell opened its

48
00:02:22,973 --> 00:02:25,354
first permanent fully electronic switching

49
00:02:25,423 --> 00:02:28,361
office, in Succasunna, New Jersey.

50
00:02:30,175 --> 00:02:31,638
What happened to the women at the

51
00:02:31,684 --> 00:02:32,718
switchboards?

52
00:02:33,228 --> 00:02:36,038
Feigenbaum and Gross compared cities that

53
00:02:36,084 --> 00:02:38,569
were cut over with cities that were not.

54
00:02:39,173 --> 00:02:41,831
Ten years later, operators in cut-over

55
00:02:41,913 --> 00:02:43,991
cities were eight percentage points less

56
00:02:44,049 --> 00:02:45,860
likely to still be operators.

57
00:02:46,464 --> 00:02:49,006
Those over twenty-five were about seven

58
00:02:49,088 --> 00:02:51,491
points less likely to be working at all.

59
00:02:52,095 --> 00:02:54,138
Many of those still working changed

60
00:02:54,184 --> 00:02:56,820
occupations: about eleven percentage

61
00:02:56,866 --> 00:02:58,828
points more often than operators

62
00:02:58,886 --> 00:02:59,583
elsewhere.

63
00:03:00,094 --> 00:03:02,672
And many who found other work landed in

64
00:03:02,788 --> 00:03:04,262
lower-paid jobs.

65
00:03:04,703 --> 00:03:07,664
Their occupation scores fell five percent,

66
00:03:07,989 --> 00:03:10,578
while their peers' rose eight.

67
00:03:12,383 --> 00:03:14,450
But the story has a second half.

68
00:03:14,891 --> 00:03:17,085
For the next generation of young women,

69
00:03:17,329 --> 00:03:19,929
operator jobs fell by fifty to eighty

70
00:03:19,999 --> 00:03:20,673
percent.

71
00:03:21,114 --> 00:03:24,109
Yet their overall employment did not fall.

72
00:03:24,550 --> 00:03:27,325
They moved into other clerical jobs and

73
00:03:27,406 --> 00:03:28,591
into service work.

74
00:03:29,032 --> 00:03:30,982
The economists put it plainly.

75
00:03:31,307 --> 00:03:33,560
Automation hurt the people already in the

76
00:03:33,618 --> 00:03:34,175
job.

77
00:03:34,616 --> 00:03:36,625
It did not close the door on those who

78
00:03:36,694 --> 00:03:37,693
came after.

79
00:03:41,208 --> 00:03:43,762
The switchboards went dark long ago.

80
00:03:44,459 --> 00:03:46,920
Down on the street, the lamplighter walks

81
00:03:47,001 --> 00:03:50,043
on, past windows where other people work

82
00:03:50,124 --> 00:03:50,612
late.

83
00:03:51,216 --> 00:03:53,828
The voice in the middle of the call never

84
00:03:53,909 --> 00:03:55,244
fully went away.

85
00:03:55,941 --> 00:03:59,830
Today, when you call a company, someone,

86
00:04:00,005 --> 00:04:02,849
or something, still answers.

87
00:04:05,508 --> 00:04:07,621
Customer service representatives are that

88
00:04:07,691 --> 00:04:08,724
voice today.

89
00:04:09,328 --> 00:04:12,381
In 2025, there were about two point seven

90
00:04:12,439 --> 00:04:14,262
million of them in the United States,

91
00:04:14,436 --> 00:04:15,922
according to the Bureau of Labor

92
00:04:15,992 --> 00:04:16,897
Statistics.

93
00:04:17,408 --> 00:04:19,870
That is about fifteen times as many as the

94
00:04:19,916 --> 00:04:23,294
telephone operators at their 1930 peak.

95
00:04:25,092 --> 00:04:27,600
And the BLS expects that number to shrink.

96
00:04:28,203 --> 00:04:32,058
Its projections for 2025 to 2035 show

97
00:04:32,174 --> 00:04:34,531
customer service jobs falling about five

98
00:04:34,577 --> 00:04:37,608
percent, to about two point five million.

99
00:04:38,211 --> 00:04:40,638
That is a projection, not a count.

100
00:04:41,241 --> 00:04:43,296
Forecasts can be wrong.

101
00:04:45,092 --> 00:04:45,777
Why?

102
00:04:46,381 --> 00:04:49,771
In a 2025 article, BLS economists

103
00:04:49,840 --> 00:04:52,453
explained how they factor in artificial

104
00:04:52,511 --> 00:04:53,393
intelligence.

105
00:04:53,834 --> 00:04:56,435
They expect it to mainly affect jobs whose

106
00:04:56,493 --> 00:04:59,082
core tasks can be most easily replicated

107
00:04:59,407 --> 00:05:02,135
by generative AI in its current form.

108
00:05:02,832 --> 00:05:04,655
Customer service was one of their

109
00:05:04,713 --> 00:05:05,537
examples.

110
00:05:05,978 --> 00:05:08,881
In that earlier projection, for 2023 to

111
00:05:08,962 --> 00:05:12,735
2033, these jobs were set to decline five

112
00:05:12,782 --> 00:05:13,420
percent.

113
00:05:14,117 --> 00:05:16,880
Medical transcriptionists, whose work also

114
00:05:16,961 --> 00:05:19,899
turns spoken words into records, were set

115
00:05:19,945 --> 00:05:22,894
to decline four point seven percent.

116
00:05:24,675 --> 00:05:27,566
What does AI actually do at the help desk?

117
00:05:28,680 --> 00:05:31,920
Economists Erik Brynjolfsson, Danielle Li

118
00:05:32,163 --> 00:05:34,567
and Lindsey Raymond studied five thousand

119
00:05:34,648 --> 00:05:37,295
one hundred seventy-nine support agents at

120
00:05:37,365 --> 00:05:40,058
one large software company, most of them

121
00:05:40,116 --> 00:05:41,614
working in the Philippines.

122
00:05:42,125 --> 00:05:44,876
With an AI assistant, agents resolved

123
00:05:44,992 --> 00:05:47,453
fourteen percent more issues per hour on

124
00:05:47,500 --> 00:05:48,162
average.

125
00:05:48,858 --> 00:05:51,169
For newer and less-skilled agents, the

126
00:05:51,227 --> 00:05:52,957
gain was thirty-four percent.

127
00:05:53,398 --> 00:05:55,731
For the most experienced, it was minimal.

128
00:05:56,242 --> 00:05:58,297
New agents with two months on the job

129
00:05:58,622 --> 00:06:01,316
performed like agents with six months,

130
00:06:01,560 --> 00:06:02,697
without the tool.

131
00:06:03,301 --> 00:06:05,762
The authors are careful: the study cannot

132
00:06:05,832 --> 00:06:08,038
show what happens to jobs overall.

133
00:06:08,549 --> 00:06:11,208
Firms might hire more newcomers, or need

134
00:06:11,277 --> 00:06:12,322
fewer of them.

135
00:06:14,133 --> 00:06:16,351
There is an early signal about who feels

136
00:06:16,409 --> 00:06:17,175
it first.

137
00:06:17,616 --> 00:06:19,787
Brynjolfsson and his Stanford colleagues

138
00:06:19,868 --> 00:06:22,202
follow payroll records for millions of

139
00:06:22,272 --> 00:06:23,502
American workers.

140
00:06:24,106 --> 00:06:27,577
Through June 2026, they find no evidence

141
00:06:27,624 --> 00:06:30,712
of widespread job loss across the economy.

142
00:06:31,153 --> 00:06:33,359
But for workers aged twenty-two to

143
00:06:33,417 --> 00:06:35,437
twenty-five in the occupations most

144
00:06:35,507 --> 00:06:38,560
exposed to AI, employment now stands

145
00:06:38,642 --> 00:06:41,231
nineteen percent below where it would have

146
00:06:41,265 --> 00:06:41,776
been.

147
00:06:42,380 --> 00:06:45,108
Older workers show no such gap.

148
00:06:45,550 --> 00:06:47,733
And it happens mostly through fewer young

149
00:06:47,791 --> 00:06:50,542
people being hired, not more people being

150
00:06:50,600 --> 00:06:51,378
let go.

151
00:06:51,982 --> 00:06:54,513
Customer service representatives are among

152
00:06:54,571 --> 00:06:56,765
the largest jobs in the most exposed

153
00:06:56,823 --> 00:06:57,346
group.

154
00:06:57,949 --> 00:07:00,387
The authors call these early, descriptive

155
00:07:00,434 --> 00:07:03,360
signals, not proof of cause.

156
00:07:05,175 --> 00:07:07,555
So here is the comparison, on the same

157
00:07:07,601 --> 00:07:10,504
scale: change over ten years.

158
00:07:11,015 --> 00:07:14,660
Between 1930 and 1940, as the dial spread,

159
00:07:14,834 --> 00:07:16,843
the number of telephone-industry operators

160
00:07:16,901 --> 00:07:18,828
fell about sixteen percent.

161
00:07:19,339 --> 00:07:21,650
For customer service representatives, the

162
00:07:21,708 --> 00:07:25,655
projected decline from 2025 to 2035 is

163
00:07:25,702 --> 00:07:26,921
about five percent.

164
00:07:27,478 --> 00:07:29,231
Not the same technology.

165
00:07:29,637 --> 00:07:32,703
The same pattern: a repetitive human task,

166
00:07:32,923 --> 00:07:35,025
taken over by an automated system.

167
00:07:35,501 --> 00:07:37,033
But the differences matter.

168
00:07:37,683 --> 00:07:40,074
Customer service is about fifteen times

169
00:07:40,144 --> 00:07:40,713
larger.

170
00:07:41,189 --> 00:07:43,848
AI reaches into office work far beyond the

171
00:07:43,883 --> 00:07:44,440
phone.

172
00:07:44,951 --> 00:07:47,191
And one of these numbers is history.

173
00:07:47,702 --> 00:07:49,711
The other is only a forecast.

174
00:07:51,508 --> 00:07:54,109
If the operators' story is any guide, the

175
00:07:54,167 --> 00:07:56,605
question is not only how many jobs.

176
00:07:56,779 --> 00:07:58,613
It is who carries the cost.

177
00:07:59,217 --> 00:08:02,328
In the 1920s and 30s, the people already

178
00:08:02,410 --> 00:08:04,349
in the job paid the most.

179
00:08:04,732 --> 00:08:07,321
The next generation found other doors.

180
00:08:07,495 --> 00:08:10,014
Today, the early signals point the other

181
00:08:10,084 --> 00:08:10,653
way.

182
00:08:11,036 --> 00:08:14,240
AI helps newcomers most on the job, yet

183
00:08:14,356 --> 00:08:16,423
fewer young people are getting through the

184
00:08:16,469 --> 00:08:17,061
door.

185
00:08:17,758 --> 00:08:20,440
It is too soon to know if that will last.

186
00:08:20,951 --> 00:08:23,424
It depends on things no chart can show

187
00:08:23,540 --> 00:08:27,324
yet: how fast the change comes, and what

188
00:08:27,440 --> 00:08:29,217
new work grows around it.

189
00:08:29,820 --> 00:08:32,374
That is what we will be watching.

190
00:08:36,375 --> 00:08:38,534
Back on the street, the lamplighter

191
00:08:38,616 --> 00:08:40,148
reaches the last post.

192
00:08:40,845 --> 00:08:44,723
The glass opens, the flame takes, and the

193
00:08:44,792 --> 00:08:46,348
corner is lit again.

194
00:08:47,161 --> 00:08:50,121
Every era has its last lamplighters.

195
00:08:50,818 --> 00:08:52,815
Some of them answer the phone.

